<PageTitle>Getting started · Linq2Dashboard</PageTitle>

<h1>Getting started</h1>

<p>In a hurry? <a href="five-minutes">Try it in five minutes</a> walks through a fresh app step by step, with the dashboard defined in the page markup. <a href="cached-dashboard">Cache a shared dashboard</a> turns that app into one dashboard built once and shared by every user, the way the builder below is meant for large data. This page is the fuller tour.</p>

<p>
    Two packages on NuGet: <code>Linq2Dashboard</code> is the engine and has no UI dependency; <code>Linq2Dashboard.Blazor</code>
    renders it and depends on the engine, so a Blazor app adds only the Blazor package. Both target .NET 10. Versions are
    prerelease while the API settles.
</p>

<pre><code>dotnet add package Linq2Dashboard.Blazor --prerelease   # a Blazor app; the engine comes with it
dotnet add package Linq2Dashboard --prerelease          # the engine alone, for a host without the components</code></pre>

<h2>1. Build a dashboard</h2>

<p>Build it once, from the collection you already have in memory. Facets, metrics and the sort order are fixed at this point; only selections change afterwards.</p>

<pre><code>@BuilderExample</code></pre>

<p>
    One thing to know before adding a <code>TextFacet</code>: it is the one potentially expensive operation. Every other facet
    counts through a column lookup, but a new text calls your function once per row in the dataset, so the cost is set by your
    function and the row count. Two case-insensitive <code>Contains</code> over a million rows take about 80 ms, or 20 ms with
    <code>EnableParallelCounting()</code> on four cores. Only a new text pays; the result is cached by the text afterwards, and the
    Blazor input waits for the user to pause before applying it. Keep the function cheap, or precompute what it reads.
</p>

<h2>2. Calculate a state</h2>

<p>The UI owns the selections. The dashboard is a pure function of them: same selections, same state.</p>

<pre><code>@CalculateExample</code></pre>

<h2>3. Render it in Blazor</h2>

<p>Register the dashboard once, for example as a singleton, and put the components inside a <code>DashboardView</code>. Every component reads the cascaded state and turns clicks into selection changes; none of them counts anything.</p>

<pre><code>@BlazorExample</code></pre>

<h2>What the components do</h2>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Component</th><th>Renders</th><th>Notable options</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>DashboardView</code></td><td>Owns selections and state; cascades them. Bindable <code>Selections</code>; <code>StateChanged</code> hands the page every new state, the initial one included, for charts of its own.</td><td><code>Formatter</code>, <code>StateChanged</code>, <code>Context</code> for your own content and grid</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ValueFacet</code></td><td>Values with counts, the null value, "Other", search. Also boolean and multi-valued facets.</td><td><code>ShowTotals</code>, <code>HideZeroCounts</code>, <code>Collapsible</code>, templates</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>RangeFacet</code></td><td>Fixed buckets as a histogram or list; optional slider.</td><td><code>ShowSlider</code>, <code>Layout</code>, <code>ShowBounds</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>DateFacet</code></td><td>Presets with counts and periods as bars.</td><td><code>ShowPresets</code>, <code>Layout</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>TextFacet</code></td><td>A debounced input whose text becomes a selection, matched by the function given in the builder.</td><td><code>DebounceMilliseconds</code>, <code>Placeholder</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ActiveSelections</code></td><td>One removable chip per selected value or interval, clear all.</td><td><code>ShowFacetName</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Metric</code></td><td>One metric tile by key, placed where you want it; a dash when no row contributed. Count, sum and distinct show their share of the total as a percentage, and a <code>Count</code> metric is the matching row count.</td><td><code>Name</code>, <code>MetricTemplate</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>StateSummary</code></td><td>Everything in the state as plain clickable lists: counts, metrics and every facet. The default content of <code>DashboardView</code>, for a first look before laying out a page.</td><td>the texts</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p>
    Metric tiles are placed one by one, so each page decides which metrics it shows, in what order and where.
    For a responsive row of tiles, wrap them in a grid:
</p>

<pre><code>.metrics {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(var(--l2d-metric-min-width, 9rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--l2d-gap, 0.375rem);
}</code></pre>

<h2>Formatting and styling</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>One formatter.</strong> An <code>IDashboardFormatter</code> cascades from <code>DashboardView</code>. Culture, number formats, the null label and preset names come from it, and from nowhere else. Derive from <code>DefaultDashboardFormatter</code> to change any of them. The one exception is a value facet with a <code>Label</code> selector: its labels come from the rows at creation and the formatter shows them as they are.</li>
    <li><strong>Custom properties.</strong> Every <code>--l2d-*</code> property is declared on <code>.l2d-dashboard</code>. Set them on that element or an ancestor to restyle without touching markup. Dark-scheme neutrals are built in.</li>
    <li><strong>Classes and attributes.</strong> Every component takes <code>Class</code>, rendered after its own <code>l2d-*</code> classes, and passes any other attribute (<code>id</code>, <code>style</code>, <code>data-*</code>) to its root element, so a page can target one facet or tile from its own stylesheet without a wrapper. State classes such as <code>l2d-selected</code>, <code>l2d-zero</code>, <code>l2d-null</code>, <code>l2d-metric-empty</code> and <code>l2d-collapsed</code> are stable hooks.</li>
    <li><strong>Templates.</strong> <code>HeaderTemplate</code> and <code>ValueTemplate</code> on the facets, <code>MetricTemplate</code> on the tiles (it replaces the whole tile, so your markup is the root).</li>
    <li><strong>Rows.</strong> The library renders no rows; they go to the grid you already use. <code>Context="dash"</code> names the view's context in your markup. The rows come in two shapes for the two kinds of grid: <code>dash.Items</code> is one <code>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</code> per state for a grid that queries its source, QuickGrid among them, and <code>dash.State.Items</code> is the same rows as a counted, indexable list for a grid that takes a list. Either reference changes exactly when the state does, so a grid that rebuilds when its source changes does so after every click and never in between; paging, virtualising, sorting and counting cost the slice, not a pass over every row. Do not hand a grid <code>ToList()</code> of either: that copies the rows on every render and gives the grid a new source every time, so it rebuilds itself on every render.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Bookmarks</h2>

<p>Selections serialise to JSON through <code>dashboard.Serializer</code>. Reading is lenient: unknown facets and values that cannot be read are dropped, so a stale bookmark degrades to fewer selections rather than an error. For a URL, <code>ToQueryString</code> and <code>FromQueryString</code> write one readable parameter per facet, such as <code>?Country=SE,NO&amp;Amount=100..500</code>; <code>SyncUrl</code> on <code>DashboardView</code> does this for you, which is how the <a href="demo">demo</a> keeps its selections in the URL.</p>
